---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Growing From Startup to Scale Without Legacy Overhead - Marker"
>
  <Navbar />
  <main id="main-content">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-16 pb-16 md:pt-24 md:pb-24">
      <a
        href="/case-studies"
        class="mb-8 inline-flex items-center text-sm text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground"
      >
        <svg
          class="mr-1 h-4 w-4"
          fill="none"
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
        >
          <path
            stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round"
            d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7"></path>
        </svg>
        All Case Studies
      </a>

      <span
        class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-primary"
      >
        Premium Activewear
      </span>
      <h1
        class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-3xl"
      >
        Growing From Startup to Scale Without Legacy Overhead
      </h1>

      <div
        class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 p-5 text-sm"
      >
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Company</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground"
              >Fast-growing premium activewear brand</span
            >
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Team</span>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground"
              >~16-person product development team managing ~50 vendors</span
            >
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block font-medium text-foreground">Key Challenge</span
            >
            <span class="text-muted-foreground"
              >Building sourcing infrastructure that scales with rapid growth
              without inheriting complexity from legacy systems</span
            >
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">The Challenge</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          This premium activewear brand had grown rapidly, reaching a headcount
          of several hundred employees while managing approximately 50 vendor
          relationships. The head of sourcing — a veteran of one of the world's
          largest apparel companies — had seen firsthand what happens when
          fast-growing brands adopt the same tools and processes that slow down
          their larger competitors.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          At his previous company, a Global Integrated Sourcing (GIS) function
          had become necessary to coordinate sourcing across regions — a massive,
          expensive organizational layer. His goal was to prevent this brand from
          ever needing one. The advantage of being smaller was the ability to
          skip steps, move quickly, and avoid the multi-system integrations that
          create friction at scale.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The sourcing leader's concern wasn't about cutting costs — the brand's
          margins were healthy. It was about visibility. As the team grew and
          took on more vendors, the risk was that costing knowledge would become
          siloed. If someone went on PTO, visibility into that part of the supply
          base went with them. And without historical cost data tracked over
          time, the team had no way to know whether they were negotiating against
          themselves.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">The Approach</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Marker gives the team the visibility of a GIS function without the
          organizational overhead. By capturing supplier communications
          centrally, the team builds a shared record of costing history,
          negotiation context, and vendor relationships — knowledge that
          previously lived only in individual inboxes.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The sourcing leader's team operates primarily during US business hours,
          communicating with vendors around the world. Marker ensures that when
          one team member is out, others can pick up the full context of a
          supplier relationship — not just the latest email, but the trajectory
          of the conversation and any commitments made.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Cost data is tracked over time across suppliers, giving the team
          trend-level visibility. When a factory's pricing drifts upward or a new
          vendor offers better terms for similar construction, the information is
          available without requiring a dedicated analysis cycle. The sourcing
          leader noted that at his previous employer, "simply having visibility
          saved a ton of money" — not through adversarial negotiation, but by
          ensuring the team had the information to make informed decisions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="mt-10">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-foreground">Key Takeaways</h2>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-muted-foreground">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"
            ></span>
            <span
              >Shared visibility across the sourcing team eliminates knowledge
              silos and PTO-driven blind spots</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"
            ></span>
            <span
              >Historical cost data tracked over time to surface pricing trends
              across vendors</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"
            ></span>
            <span
              >Full context continuity when team members hand off or are
              unavailable — relationships don't go dark</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"
            ></span>
            <span
              >Lightweight infrastructure that avoids the complexity of
              enterprise PLM and GIS systems</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
            <span class="mt-1.5 block h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary"
            ></span>
            <span
              >Sourcing decisions informed by data visibility rather than
              adversarial cost-cutting</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section
        class="mt-12 rounded-lg border border-primary/20 bg-primary/5 p-6 text-center"
      >
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-foreground">
          See how Marker helps growing brands scale sourcing without adding
          complexity.
        </p>
        <a
          href="/book-demo"
          class="mt-4 inline-flex h-10 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-primary px-6 text-sm font-medium text-primary-foreground transition-colors hover:bg-primary/90 focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-ring focus-visible:ring-offset-2"
        >
          Book a Demo
        </a>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
